('state-machine','advanced','What is a redundant state?',ARRAY['State that can be merged with another','State with no inputs','State with no outputs','Final state'],0,'Redundant states are equivalent and can be merged.',20),
('state-machine','advanced','What is the implication chart method?',ARRAY['State minimization technique','State encoding method','State diagram method','State table method'],0,'Implication chart: pairwise state equivalence testing.',20),
('state-machine','advanced','What is a state graph traversal for verification?',ARRAY['Model checking all reachable states','Simulation only','Testing only','Formal proof only'],0,'Model checking: exhaustively verify FSM properties.',20),
('state-machine','advanced','What is SPIN model checker?',ARRAY['Tool for verifying FSM properties','Circuit simulator','State encoder','K-map tool'],0,'SPIN: verifies LTL/never-claims on Promela models.',20),
('state-machine','advanced','What is LTL (Linear Temporal Logic)?',ARRAY['Logic for expressing time-dependent properties','Logic for circuits','Logic for gates','Logic for states'],0,'LTL: □ (always), ◇ (eventually), ○ (next), U (until).',20),
('state-machine','advanced','What is CTL (Computation Tree Logic)?',ARRAY['Logic for branching time properties','Linear time logic','No temporal logic','State logic'],0,'CTL: A (all paths), E (exists path), G (globally), F (future).',20),
('state-machine','advanced','What is the difference between LTL and CTL?',ARRAY['LTL: linear time; CTL: branching time','No difference','LTL is stronger','CTL is weaker'],0,'LTL: one path at a time. CTL: quantifies over paths.',20),
('state-machine','advanced','What is a fairness constraint?',ARRAY['Condition that must hold infinitely often','Condition that holds once','No condition','Reset condition'],0,'Fairness: e.g., ''request always eventually granted.''',20),
('state-machine','advanced','What is a deadlock-free FSM?',ARRAY['No state with no outgoing transitions','All states have inputs','All states have outputs','All states reachable'],0,'Deadlock-free: every state has at least one valid transition.',20),
